{
  "requirement": "`habeas setup` MUST configure native nextest settings in\n`.config/nextest.toml` at the Cargo workspace root so the coverage workflow of\n{cite}`requirements.test-coverage.run` routes each executed test attempt through\nthe per-process collector of {cite}`requirements.test-coverage.processes`.\nThose settings MUST use nextest's own configuration format.\nRepeating setup MUST avoid duplicate configuration, preserve unrelated nextest\nsettings, and report conflicting settings for review rather than overwrite them.",
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "I judge the reviewed tests adequate for `requirements.test-coverage.setup.configuration`. I read `configure` and `check` in `src/execution_coverage/configuration.rs` only to see what the tests exercise. The expected behaviour comes from the requirement text.\n\n**Obligations and the tests that cover them**\n\n1. **Configuration lives in `.config/nextest.toml` at the Cargo workspace root.**\n   - `workspace_member_uses_the_native_config_at_the_workspace_root` configures from a member and asserts the path is the workspace root's `.config/nextest.toml`. It asserts nothing is created under the member or `target`. It also covers an excluded member, which becomes its own root.\n   - `explicit_workspace_pointer_places_configuration_at_a_sibling_workspace` covers a `workspace = '../workspace'` pointer.\n   - `native_settings_inheritance_and_precedence` and the tests in `tests/setup.rs` cover the plain single-root case.\n\n2. **Settings route each executed attempt through the per-process collector.**\n   - The generated source must pass `check` for the `ci` and `default` profiles. `check` requires `experimental` to include `wrapper-scripts`, and requires `scripts.wrapper.habeas-coverage` to be the `[\"habeas\", \"coverage-process\"]` command with `target-runner = \"within-wrapper\"`. It also requires the first `run-wrapper` rule to be unconditional (`filter = 'all()'`, no `platform`).\n   - `native_settings_inheritance_and_precedence` pins this down with many variants. Inherited profiles are accepted. A narrowing filter, a `platform` condition, a different wrapper, or a wrong wrapper command all fail. So do a wrong `target-runner`, a self-inheritance cycle, and a rule order that puts another wrapper first.\n   - `tests/setup.rs` asserts the written file contains `coverage-process`.\n\n3. **Settings use nextest's own configuration format.**\n   - The marked tests check the key names and structure only through the project's own `check`, which is a self-consistency check.\n   - Real-nextest tests in `tests/execution_coverage.rs` give corroborating evidence. These include `real_parallel_processes_retries_child_profiles_and_filtered_reruns`. Their `fixture` writes the config through `common::coverage_setup`, which calls `habeas::execution_coverage::configure`. They then run real `cargo nextest` with retries and parallelism, and each attempt yields collector profiles.\n   - Those tests are marked for `run` and `processes`, not for this requirement. I treat that as corroboration, not a gap.\n\n4. **Repeating setup avoids duplicates.**\n   - `native_settings_inheritance_and_precedence` asserts `configure` run on an already-configured file returns identical source.\n   - `coverage_setup_is_idempotent_and_hook_needs_no_coverage_chapter` runs `habeas setup` twice and asserts the config is byte-identical.\n\n5. **Setup preserves unrelated nextest settings.**\n   - `native_settings_inheritance_and_precedence` starts from a file with `experimental = ['setup-scripts']`, `retries = 2`, and a `setup = 'prepare'` script rule. It asserts all three survive in the planned output.\n\n6. **Setup reports conflicts for review instead of overwriting.**\n   - `setup_conflicts_name_the_setting_and_preserve_existing_native_configuration` covers six conflicts:\n     - a changed wrapper command;\n     - a changed `target-runner`;\n     - a non-array `experimental`;\n     - a non-table `scripts`;\n     - a non-array `profile.default.scripts`;\n     - an existing conflicting `run-wrapper` rule.\n   - For each, it asserts the error names the setting and `.config/nextest.toml`, and that the file content is unchanged.\n   - `setup_reports_a_conflicting_nextest_wrapper_without_writing_project_files` runs the real CLI. It asserts a failing exit status, a stderr that names the file and setting, and that the nextest file, `book.toml`, `SUMMARY.md` and `Cargo.toml` are unchanged. It also asserts no other project files are created.\n   - `coverage_setup_is_idempotent_and_hook_needs_no_coverage_chapter` asserts a conflicting file is left untouched.\n\n**Residual note.** The marked tests validate the nextest key names mainly through the project's own `check`. Real-nextest acceptance is exercised only by tests marked for other requirements. I consider that corroboration, not an adequacy gap. I found no plausible violation of the requirement that the marked tests would accept.",
  "references": "96b720306e7c96652a5898cfb92a56832cb17c63aaeae24e722eaab32c461e25",
